They can feel the same because remembering and imagining use overlapping mental machinery: both can bring back images, sounds, emotions, and a sense of “being there.” And memory isn’t a perfect recording. Each time you recall an event, your mind rebuilds it from stored pieces and what you know now.

The main difference is the source:

* A memory is a reconstruction anchored to something you experienced.
* An imagination is a construction assembled without that specific experience as its source.

But the feeling of vividness, confidence, or emotional force doesn’t reliably tell you which one it is. A vivid imagined scene can feel familiar; a real memory can feel hazy. And details can blend: you might remember the original event, then later fold in a story someone told, a photo, or something you pictured.

Our leaf image is a small example: the original photo showed a backlit leaf. The glowing molecular details were added afterward. If you saw the edited image again later, those additions might feel familiar—but that wouldn’t mean they were in the original photo.

When it matters, it helps to ask: “Do I remember experiencing this, or do I remember picturing or hearing it?” Then check any outside record you have. That won’t make memory perfect, but it can help separate the event from what got added later.